1
00:00:04,159 --> 00:00:09,477
The pale young gentleman and I stood contemplating one another in Barnard’s Inn, until we both burst

2
00:00:09,478 --> 00:00:15,559
out laughing. “The idea of its being you!” said he. “The idea of its being you!” said

3
00:00:15,559 --> 00:00:21,199
I. And then we contemplated one another afresh, and laughed again. “Well!” said

4
00:00:21,199 --> 00:00:27,159
the pale young gentleman, reaching out his hand good-humouredly, “it’s all over now, I hope,

5
00:00:27,159 --> 00:00:32,319
and it will be magnanimous in you if you’ll forgive me for having knocked you about so.”

6
00:00:32,319 --> 00:00:37,840
I derived from this speech that Mr. Herbert Pocket (for Herbert was the pale young gentleman’s name)

7
00:00:38,438 --> 00:00:44,319
still rather confounded his intention with his execution. But I made a modest reply,

8
00:00:44,319 --> 00:00:47,199
and we shook hands warmly.

9
00:00:47,199 --> 00:00:52,039
“You hadn’t come into your good fortune at that time?” said Herbert Pocket.

10
00:00:52,039 --> 00:00:54,039
“No,” said I.

11
00:00:54,039 --> 00:00:58,118
“No,” he acquiesced: “I heard it had happened very lately. I

12
00:00:58,118 --> 00:01:01,199
was rather on the lookout for good fortune then.”

13
00:01:01,199 --> 00:01:02,679
“Indeed?”

14
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:05,000
“Yes. Miss Havisham had sent for me,

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,520
to see if she could take a fancy to me. But she couldn’t,—at all events, she didn’t.”

16
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:15,879
I thought it polite to remark that I was surprised to hear that.

17
00:01:15,879 --> 00:01:23,319
“Bad taste,” said Herbert, laughing, “but a fact. Yes, she had sent for me on a trial visit,

18
00:01:23,319 --> 00:01:28,477
and if I had come out of it successfully, I suppose I should have been provided for;

19
00:01:28,478 --> 00:01:33,319
perhaps I should have been what-you-may-called it to Estella.”

20
00:01:33,319 --> 00:01:36,840
“What’s that?” I asked, with sudden gravity.

21
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:41,199
He was arranging his fruit in plates while we talked, which divided his attention,

22
00:01:41,199 --> 00:01:47,319
and was the cause of his having made this lapse of a word. “Affianced,” he explained,

23
00:01:47,319 --> 00:01:55,438
still busy with the fruit. “Betrothed. Engaged. What’s-his-named. Any word of that sort.”

24
00:01:55,438 --> 00:01:58,398
“How did you bear your disappointment?” I asked.

25
00:01:58,398 --> 00:02:03,797
“Pooh!” said he, “I didn’t care much for it. She’s a Tartar.”

26
00:02:03,798 --> 00:02:05,599
“Miss Havisham?”

27
00:02:05,599 --> 00:02:10,840
“I don’t say no to that, but I meant Estella. That girl’s hard and haughty and capricious to

28
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:17,199
the last degree, and has been brought up by Miss Havisham to wreak revenge on all the male sex.”

29
00:02:17,199 --> 00:02:20,080
“What relation is she to Miss Havisham?”

30
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:23,840
“None,” said he. “Only adopted.”

31
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:28,118
“Why should she wreak revenge on all the male sex? What revenge?”

32
00:02:28,118 --> 00:02:32,398
“Lord, Mr. Pip!” said he. “Don’t you know?”

33
00:02:32,398 --> 00:02:34,118
“No,” said I.

34
00:02:34,118 --> 00:02:37,638
“Dear me! It’s quite a story, and shall be saved till dinner-time.

35
00:02:38,278 --> 00:02:44,039
And now let me take the liberty of asking you a question. How did you come there, that day?”

36
00:02:44,039 --> 00:02:49,319
I told him, and he was attentive until I had finished, and then burst out laughing again,

37
00:02:49,319 --> 00:02:52,519
and asked me if I was sore afterwards? I didn’t

38
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:57,359
ask him if he was, for my conviction on that point was perfectly established.

39
00:02:57,359 --> 00:03:00,598
“Mr. Jaggers is your guardian, I understand?” he went on.

40
00:03:00,599 --> 00:03:01,520
“Yes.”

41
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:04,520
“You know he is Miss Havisham’s man of business and solicitor,

42
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:07,798
and has her confidence when nobody else has?”

43
00:03:07,798 --> 00:03:12,199
This was bringing me (I felt) towards dangerous ground. I

44
00:03:12,199 --> 00:03:16,039
answered with a constraint I made no attempt to disguise,

45
00:03:16,039 --> 00:03:20,798
that I had seen Mr. Jaggers in Miss Havisham’s house on the very day of our combat, but never

46
00:03:20,798 --> 00:03:26,359
at any other time, and that I believed he had no recollection of having ever seen me there.

47
00:03:26,359 --> 00:03:30,199
“He was so obliging as to suggest my father for your tutor, and he called

48
00:03:30,199 --> 00:03:34,759
on my father to propose it. Of course he knew about my father from his connection

49
00:03:34,759 --> 00:03:39,319
with Miss Havisham. My father is Miss Havisham’s cousin; not that that implies

50
00:03:39,319 --> 00:03:44,840
familiar intercourse between them, for he is a bad courtier and will not propitiate her.”

51
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:49,520
Herbert Pocket had a frank and easy way with him that was very taking. I had never seen

52
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:54,438
any one then, and I have never seen any one since, who more strongly expressed to me,

53
00:03:54,438 --> 00:04:00,718
in every look and tone, a natural incapacity to do anything secret and mean. There was something

54
00:04:00,719 --> 00:04:06,039
wonderfully hopeful about his general air, and something that at the same time whispered to me

55
00:04:06,039 --> 00:04:13,638
he would never be very successful or rich. I don’t know how this was. I became imbued with

56
00:04:13,638 --> 00:04:21,319
the notion on that first occasion before we sat down to dinner, but I cannot define by what means.

57
00:04:21,319 --> 00:04:26,398
He was still a pale young gentleman, and had a certain conquered languor about him in the

58
00:04:26,398 --> 00:04:33,118
midst of his spirits and briskness, that did not seem indicative of natural strength. He had

59
00:04:33,118 --> 00:04:37,798
not a handsome face, but it was better than handsome: being extremely amiable

60
00:04:37,798 --> 00:04:43,199
and cheerful. His figure was a little ungainly, as in the days when my knuckles had taken such

61
00:04:43,199 --> 00:04:49,519
liberties with it, but it looked as if it would always be light and young. Whether Mr. Trabb’s

62
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:56,199
local work would have sat more gracefully on him than on me, may be a question; but I am conscious

63
00:04:56,199 --> 00:05:02,519
that he carried off his rather old clothes much better than I carried off my new suit.

64
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:08,039
As he was so communicative, I felt that reserve on my part would be a bad return unsuited to

65
00:05:08,039 --> 00:05:14,719
our years. I therefore told him my small story, and laid stress on my being forbidden to inquire

66
00:05:14,719 --> 00:05:20,398
who my benefactor was. I further mentioned that as I had been brought up a blacksmith

67
00:05:20,398 --> 00:05:26,039
in a country place, and knew very little of the ways of politeness, I would take it as a great

68
00:05:26,039 --> 00:05:33,638
kindness in him if he would give me a hint whenever he saw me at a loss or going wrong.

69
00:05:33,638 --> 00:05:39,559
“With pleasure,” said he, “though I venture to prophesy that you’ll want very few hints.

70
00:05:39,559 --> 00:05:44,240
I dare say we shall be often together, and I should like to banish any needless restraint

71
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:50,879
between us. Will you do me the favour to begin at once to call me by my Christian name, Herbert?”

72
00:05:50,879 --> 00:05:57,159
I thanked him and said I would. I informed him in exchange that my Christian name was Philip.

73
00:05:57,159 --> 00:06:01,240
“I don’t take to Philip,” said he, smiling, “for it sounds like a moral boy out of the

74
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:06,798
spelling-book, who was so lazy that he fell into a pond, or so fat that he couldn’t see out of his

75
00:06:06,798 --> 00:06:12,719
eyes, or so avaricious that he locked up his cake till the mice ate it, or so determined to

76
00:06:12,719 --> 00:06:19,039
go a bird’s-nesting that he got himself eaten by bears who lived handy in the neighbourhood. I tell

77
00:06:19,039 --> 00:06:26,159
you what I should like. We are so harmonious, and you have been a blacksmith,—would you mind it?”

78
00:06:26,159 --> 00:06:32,118
“I shouldn’t mind anything that you propose,” I answered, “but I don’t understand you.”

79
00:06:32,118 --> 00:06:36,638
“Would you mind Handel for a familiar name? There’s a charming piece of music by Handel,

80
00:06:36,638 --> 00:06:39,159
called the Harmonious Blacksmith.”

81
00:06:39,159 --> 00:06:41,719
“I should like it very much.”

82
00:06:41,719 --> 00:06:47,000
“Then, my dear Handel,” said he, turning round as the door opened, “here is the dinner,

83
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,199
and I must beg of you to take the top of the table, because the dinner is of your providing.”

84
00:06:52,199 --> 00:06:57,319
This I would not hear of, so he took the top, and I faced him. It was a nice little

85
00:06:57,319 --> 00:07:03,039
dinner,—seemed to me then a very Lord Mayor’s Feast,—and it acquired additional relish from

86
00:07:03,039 --> 00:07:08,599
being eaten under those independent circumstances, with no old people by,

87
00:07:08,599 --> 00:07:14,520
and with London all around us. This again was heightened by a certain gypsy character that

88
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:19,879
set the banquet off; for while the table was, as Mr. Pumblechook might have said,

89
00:07:19,879 --> 00:07:25,559
the lap of luxury,—being entirely furnished forth from the coffee-house,—the circumjacent

90
00:07:25,559 --> 00:07:31,599
region of sitting-room was of a comparatively pastureless and shifty character; imposing on

91
00:07:31,599 --> 00:07:37,398
the waiter the wandering habits of putting the covers on the floor (where he fell over them),

92
00:07:37,398 --> 00:07:43,958
the melted butter in the arm-chair, the bread on the bookshelves, the cheese in the coal-scuttle,

93
00:07:43,958 --> 00:07:48,840
and the boiled fowl into my bed in the next room,—where I found much of its parsley and

94
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:55,638
butter in a state of congelation when I retired for the night. All this made the feast delightful,

95
00:07:55,638 --> 00:08:00,278
and when the waiter was not there to watch me, my pleasure was without alloy.

96
00:08:00,278 --> 00:08:02,398
We had made some progress in the dinner,

97
00:08:02,398 --> 00:08:06,359
when I reminded Herbert of his promise to tell me about Miss Havisham.

98
00:08:06,359 --> 00:08:11,199
“True,” he replied. “I’ll redeem it at once. Let me introduce the topic,

99
00:08:11,199 --> 00:08:15,359
Handel, by mentioning that in London it is not the custom to put the knife in

100
00:08:15,359 --> 00:08:21,000
the mouth,—for fear of accidents,—and that while the fork is reserved for that use,

101
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:26,559
it is not put further in than necessary. It is scarcely worth mentioning, only it’s as

102
00:08:26,559 --> 00:08:32,359
well to do as other people do. Also, the spoon is not generally used over-hand,

103
00:08:32,359 --> 00:08:39,838
but under. This has two advantages. You get at your mouth better (which after all is the object),

104
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:45,359
and you save a good deal of the attitude of opening oysters, on the part of the right elbow.”

105
00:08:45,359 --> 00:08:48,558
He offered these friendly suggestions in such a lively way,

106
00:08:48,559 --> 00:08:51,719
that we both laughed and I scarcely blushed.

107
00:08:51,719 --> 00:08:57,000
“Now,” he pursued, “concerning Miss Havisham. Miss Havisham, you must know,

108
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:03,398
was a spoilt child. Her mother died when she was a baby, and her father denied her nothing. Her

109
00:09:03,398 --> 00:09:08,477
father was a country gentleman down in your part of the world, and was a brewer. I don’t know why

110
00:09:08,478 --> 00:09:14,000
it should be a crack thing to be a brewer; but it is indisputable that while you cannot possibly be

111
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:20,798
genteel and bake, you may be as genteel as never was and brew. You see it every day.”

112
00:09:20,798 --> 00:09:25,080
“Yet a gentleman may not keep a public-house; may he?” said I.

113
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:30,477
“Not on any account,” returned Herbert; “but a public-house may keep a gentleman.

114
00:09:30,478 --> 00:09:36,000
Well! Mr. Havisham was very rich and very proud. So was his daughter.”

115
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,680
“Miss Havisham was an only child?” I hazarded.

116
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:44,798
“Stop a moment, I am coming to that. No, she was not an only child;

117
00:09:44,798 --> 00:09:52,080
she had a half-brother. Her father privately married again—his cook, I rather think.”

118
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:54,759
“I thought he was proud,” said I.

119
00:09:54,759 --> 00:10:00,638
“My good Handel, so he was. He married his second wife privately, because he was proud,

120
00:10:00,638 --> 00:10:06,638
and in course of time she died. When she was dead, I apprehend he first told his daughter what he

121
00:10:06,638 --> 00:10:12,199
had done, and then the son became a part of the family, residing in the house you are acquainted

122
00:10:12,199 --> 00:10:20,118
with. As the son grew a young man, he turned out riotous, extravagant, undutiful,—altogether

123
00:10:20,118 --> 00:10:28,359
bad. At last his father disinherited him; but he softened when he was dying, and left him well off,

124
00:10:28,359 --> 00:10:34,199
though not nearly so well off as Miss Havisham.—Take another glass of wine,

125
00:10:34,199 --> 00:10:39,438
and excuse my mentioning that society as a body does not expect one to be so strictly

126
00:10:39,438 --> 00:10:46,398
conscientious in emptying one’s glass, as to turn it bottom upwards with the rim on one’s nose.”

127
00:10:46,398 --> 00:10:52,519
I had been doing this, in an excess of attention to his recital. I thanked him, and apologised.

128
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:58,000
He said, “Not at all,” and resumed. “Miss Havisham was now an heiress,

129
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:03,879
and you may suppose was looked after as a great match. Her half-brother had now ample means again,

130
00:11:03,879 --> 00:11:09,520
but what with debts and what with new madness wasted them most fearfully again. There were

131
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:14,520
stronger differences between him and her than there had been between him and his father,

132
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:19,039
and it is suspected that he cherished a deep and mortal grudge against her as

133
00:11:19,039 --> 00:11:25,559
having influenced the father’s anger. Now, I come to the cruel part of the story,—merely

134
00:11:25,559 --> 00:11:32,478
breaking off, my dear Handel, to remark that a dinner-napkin will not go into a tumbler.”

135
00:11:32,478 --> 00:11:37,040
Why I was trying to pack mine into my tumbler, I am wholly unable to say.

136
00:11:37,719 --> 00:11:42,478
I only know that I found myself, with a perseverance worthy of a much better cause,

137
00:11:42,478 --> 00:11:47,759
making the most strenuous exertions to compress it within those limits. Again I thanked him and

138
00:11:47,759 --> 00:11:55,240
apologised, and again he said in the cheerfullest manner, “Not at all, I am sure!” and resumed.

139
00:11:55,240 --> 00:11:58,519
“There appeared upon the scene—say at the races, or the public balls,

140
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:04,879
or anywhere else you like—a certain man, who made love to Miss Havisham. I never saw him (for this

141
00:12:04,879 --> 00:12:11,319
happened five-and-twenty years ago, before you and I were, Handel), but I have heard my father

142
00:12:11,319 --> 00:12:18,080
mention that he was a showy man, and the kind of man for the purpose. But that he was not to be,

143
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:25,359
without ignorance or prejudice, mistaken for a gentleman, my father most strongly asseverates;

144
00:12:25,359 --> 00:12:31,278
because it is a principle of his that no man who was not a true gentleman at heart ever was, since

145
00:12:31,278 --> 00:12:39,519
the world began, a true gentleman in manner. He says, no varnish can hide the grain of the wood;

146
00:12:39,520 --> 00:12:46,879
and that the more varnish you put on, the more the grain will express itself. Well! This man

147
00:12:46,879 --> 00:12:52,840
pursued Miss Havisham closely, and professed to be devoted to her. I believe she had not shown

148
00:12:52,840 --> 00:12:58,478
much susceptibility up to that time; but all the susceptibility she possessed certainly came

149
00:12:58,478 --> 00:13:05,438
out then, and she passionately loved him. There is no doubt that she perfectly idolized him. He

150
00:13:05,438 --> 00:13:11,477
practised on her affection in that systematic way, that he got great sums of money from her,

151
00:13:11,478 --> 00:13:16,958
and he induced her to buy her brother out of a share in the brewery (which had been weakly left

152
00:13:16,958 --> 00:13:23,680
him by his father) at an immense price, on the plea that when he was her husband he must hold

153
00:13:23,680 --> 00:13:30,958
and manage it all. Your guardian was not at that time in Miss Havisham’s counsels, and she was too

154
00:13:30,958 --> 00:13:36,919
haughty and too much in love to be advised by any one. Her relations were poor and scheming,

155
00:13:37,638 --> 00:13:44,278
with the exception of my father; he was poor enough, but not time-serving or jealous. The

156
00:13:44,278 --> 00:13:49,039
only independent one among them, he warned her that she was doing too much for this man,

157
00:13:49,039 --> 00:13:55,519
and was placing herself too unreservedly in his power. She took the first opportunity of angrily

158
00:13:55,520 --> 00:14:02,478
ordering my father out of the house, in his presence, and my father has never seen her since.”

159
00:14:02,478 --> 00:14:07,159
I thought of her having said, “Matthew will come and see me at last when I am laid dead

160
00:14:07,159 --> 00:14:13,879
upon that table;” and I asked Herbert whether his father was so inveterate against her?

161
00:14:13,879 --> 00:14:20,398
“It’s not that,” said he, “but she charged him, in the presence of her intended husband, with being

162
00:14:20,398 --> 00:14:25,958
disappointed in the hope of fawning upon her for his own advancement, and, if he were to go to her

163
00:14:25,958 --> 00:14:35,199
now, it would look true—even to him—and even to her. To return to the man and make an end of him.

164
00:14:35,199 --> 00:14:41,159
The marriage day was fixed, the wedding dresses were bought, the wedding tour was planned out,

165
00:14:41,159 --> 00:14:47,919
the wedding guests were invited. The day came, but not the bridegroom. He wrote her a letter—”

166
00:14:47,919 --> 00:14:50,319
“Which she received,” I struck in,

167
00:14:50,319 --> 00:14:54,840
“when she was dressing for her marriage? At twenty minutes to nine?”

168
00:14:54,840 --> 00:15:00,319
“At the hour and minute,” said Herbert, nodding, “at which she afterwards stopped all the clocks.

169
00:15:00,319 --> 00:15:05,159
What was in it, further than that it most heartlessly broke the marriage off, I can’t

170
00:15:05,159 --> 00:15:11,080
tell you, because I don’t know. When she recovered from a bad illness that she had, she laid the

171
00:15:11,080 --> 00:15:17,159
whole place waste, as you have seen it, and she has never since looked upon the light of day.”

172
00:15:17,159 --> 00:15:21,319
“Is that all the story?” I asked, after considering it.

173
00:15:21,319 --> 00:15:27,359
“All I know of it; and indeed I only know so much, through piecing it out for myself; for

174
00:15:27,359 --> 00:15:33,118
my father always avoids it, and, even when Miss Havisham invited me to go there, told me no more

175
00:15:33,118 --> 00:15:40,438
of it than it was absolutely requisite I should understand. But I have forgotten one thing. It has

176
00:15:40,438 --> 00:15:46,000
been supposed that the man to whom she gave her misplaced confidence acted throughout in concert

177
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:53,118
with her half-brother; that it was a conspiracy between them; and that they shared the profits.”

178
00:15:53,118 --> 00:15:56,638
“I wonder he didn’t marry her and get all the property,” said I.

179
00:15:56,638 --> 00:16:00,039
“He may have been married already, and her cruel mortification may have been a part

180
00:16:00,039 --> 00:16:05,438
of her half-brother’s scheme,” said Herbert. “Mind! I don’t know that.”

181
00:16:05,438 --> 00:16:10,240
“What became of the two men?” I asked, after again considering the subject.

182
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:16,278
“They fell into deeper shame and degradation—if there can be deeper—and ruin.”

183
00:16:16,278 --> 00:16:18,438
“Are they alive now?”

184
00:16:18,438 --> 00:16:20,519
“I don’t know.”

185
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:26,599
“You said just now that Estella was not related to Miss Havisham, but adopted. When adopted?”

186
00:16:26,599 --> 00:16:30,840
Herbert shrugged his shoulders. “There has always been an Estella,

187
00:16:30,840 --> 00:16:38,199
since I have heard of a Miss Havisham. I know no more. And now, Handel,” said he,

188
00:16:38,199 --> 00:16:44,477
finally throwing off the story as it were, “there is a perfectly open understanding between us. All

189
00:16:44,478 --> 00:16:51,759
that I know about Miss Havisham, you know.” “And all that I know,” I retorted, “you know.”

190
00:16:51,759 --> 00:16:56,000
“I fully believe it. So there can be no competition or perplexity between

191
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:02,680
you and me. And as to the condition on which you hold your advancement in life,—namely,

192
00:17:02,680 --> 00:17:09,520
that you are not to inquire or discuss to whom you owe it,—you may be very sure that it will never

193
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:16,278
be encroached upon, or even approached, by me, or by any one belonging to me.”

194
00:17:16,278 --> 00:17:22,199
In truth, he said this with so much delicacy, that I felt the subject done with,

195
00:17:22,199 --> 00:17:27,000
even though I should be under his father’s roof for years and years to come. Yet he

196
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:31,118
said it with so much meaning, too, that I felt he as perfectly understood Miss

197
00:17:31,118 --> 00:17:36,879
Havisham to be my benefactress, as I understood the fact myself.

198
00:17:36,879 --> 00:17:41,398
It had not occurred to me before, that he had led up to the theme for the purpose of clearing

199
00:17:41,398 --> 00:17:46,359
it out of our way; but we were so much the lighter and easier for having broached it,

200
00:17:46,359 --> 00:17:51,118
that I now perceived this to be the case. We were very gay and sociable,

201
00:17:51,118 --> 00:17:56,199
and I asked him, in the course of conversation, what he was? He replied,

202
00:17:56,199 --> 00:18:01,840
“A capitalist,—an Insurer of Ships.” I suppose he saw me glancing about the

203
00:18:01,840 --> 00:18:09,039
room in search of some tokens of Shipping, or capital, for he added, “In the City.”

204
00:18:09,039 --> 00:18:14,559
I had grand ideas of the wealth and importance of Insurers of Ships in the City, and I began to

205
00:18:14,559 --> 00:18:20,638
think with awe of having laid a young Insurer on his back, blackened his enterprising eye,

206
00:18:20,638 --> 00:18:27,080
and cut his responsible head open. But again there came upon me, for my relief,

207
00:18:27,080 --> 00:18:32,199
that odd impression that Herbert Pocket would never be very successful or rich.

208
00:18:32,199 --> 00:18:36,637
“I shall not rest satisfied with merely employing my capital in insuring ships.

209
00:18:37,319 --> 00:18:43,039
I shall buy up some good Life Assurance shares, and cut into the Direction. I shall also do a

210
00:18:43,039 --> 00:18:48,319
little in the mining way. None of these things will interfere with my chartering a few thousand

211
00:18:48,319 --> 00:18:54,718
tons on my own account. I think I shall trade,” said he, leaning back in his chair, “to the East

212
00:18:54,719 --> 00:19:03,840
Indies, for silks, shawls, spices, dyes, drugs, and precious woods. It’s an interesting trade.”

213
00:19:03,840 --> 00:19:06,599
“And the profits are large?” said I.

214
00:19:06,599 --> 00:19:09,080
“Tremendous!” said he.

215
00:19:09,080 --> 00:19:13,958
I wavered again, and began to think here were greater expectations than my own.

216
00:19:13,958 --> 00:19:18,599
“I think I shall trade, also,” said he, putting his thumbs in his waist-coat pockets,

217
00:19:18,599 --> 00:19:26,478
“to the West Indies, for sugar, tobacco, and rum. Also to Ceylon, especially for elephants’ tusks.”

218
00:19:26,478 --> 00:19:29,478
“You will want a good many ships,” said I.

219
00:19:29,478 --> 00:19:32,199
“A perfect fleet,” said he.

220
00:19:32,199 --> 00:19:36,359
Quite overpowered by the magnificence of these transactions, I asked him where

221
00:19:36,359 --> 00:19:39,797
the ships he insured mostly traded to at present?

222
00:19:39,798 --> 00:19:45,159
“I haven’t begun insuring yet,” he replied. “I am looking about me.”

223
00:19:45,159 --> 00:19:47,558
Somehow, that pursuit seemed more in keeping

224
00:19:47,559 --> 00:19:54,039
with Barnard’s Inn. I said (in a tone of conviction), “Ah-h!”

225
00:19:54,039 --> 00:19:57,278
“Yes. I am in a counting-house, and looking about me.”

226
00:19:57,278 --> 00:20:00,319
“Is a counting-house profitable?” I asked.

227
00:20:00,319 --> 00:20:05,118
“To—do you mean to the young fellow who’s in it?” he asked, in reply.

228
00:20:05,118 --> 00:20:06,519
“Yes; to you.”

229
00:20:07,080 --> 00:20:11,680
“Why, n-no; not to me.” He said this with the air of one carefully

230
00:20:11,680 --> 00:20:16,840
reckoning up and striking a balance. “Not directly profitable. That is,

231
00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:21,520
it doesn’t pay me anything, and I have to—keep myself.”

232
00:20:21,520 --> 00:20:27,118
This certainly had not a profitable appearance, and I shook my head as if I would imply that it

233
00:20:27,118 --> 00:20:32,398
would be difficult to lay by much accumulative capital from such a source of income.

234
00:20:32,398 --> 00:20:37,398
“But the thing is,” said Herbert Pocket, “that you look about you. That’s the grand

235
00:20:37,398 --> 00:20:41,919
thing. You are in a counting-house, you know, and you look about you.”

236
00:20:41,919 --> 00:20:45,919
It struck me as a singular implication that you couldn’t be out of a counting-house,

237
00:20:45,919 --> 00:20:51,759
you know, and look about you; but I silently deferred to his experience.

238
00:20:51,759 --> 00:20:56,919
“Then the time comes,” said Herbert, “when you see your opening. And you go in,

239
00:20:56,919 --> 00:21:03,000
and you swoop upon it and you make your capital, and then there you are! When

240
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:08,599
you have once made your capital, you have nothing to do but employ it.”

241
00:21:08,599 --> 00:21:14,039
This was very like his way of conducting that encounter in the garden; very like.

242
00:21:14,039 --> 00:21:18,719
His manner of bearing his poverty, too, exactly corresponded to his manner of

243
00:21:18,719 --> 00:21:24,000
bearing that defeat. It seemed to me that he took all blows and buffets now with just the

244
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:28,319
same air as he had taken mine then. It was evident that he had nothing around

245
00:21:28,319 --> 00:21:32,319
him but the simplest necessaries, for everything that I remarked upon turned

246
00:21:32,319 --> 00:21:36,438
out to have been sent in on my account from the coffee-house or somewhere else.

247
00:21:37,359 --> 00:21:42,598
Yet, having already made his fortune in his own mind, he was so unassuming with it that I

248
00:21:42,599 --> 00:21:48,080
felt quite grateful to him for not being puffed up. It was a pleasant addition to his naturally

249
00:21:48,080 --> 00:21:54,477
pleasant ways, and we got on famously. In the evening we went out for a walk in the streets,

250
00:21:54,478 --> 00:22:00,240
and went half-price to the Theatre; and next day we went to church at Westminster Abbey, and in the

251
00:22:00,240 --> 00:22:06,359
afternoon we walked in the Parks; and I wondered who shod all the horses there, and wished Joe did.

252
00:22:07,559 --> 00:22:13,719
On a moderate computation, it was many months, that Sunday, since I had left Joe and Biddy.

253
00:22:13,719 --> 00:22:18,559
The space interposed between myself and them partook of that expansion, and our marshes

254
00:22:18,559 --> 00:22:25,118
were any distance off. That I could have been at our old church in my old church-going clothes,

255
00:22:25,118 --> 00:22:31,359
on the very last Sunday that ever was, seemed a combination of impossibilities, geographical and

256
00:22:31,359 --> 00:22:39,080
social, solar and lunar. Yet in the London streets so crowded with people and so brilliantly lighted

257
00:22:39,080 --> 00:22:44,638
in the dusk of evening, there were depressing hints of reproaches for that I had put the poor

258
00:22:44,638 --> 00:22:51,879
old kitchen at home so far away; and in the dead of night, the footsteps of some incapable impostor

259
00:22:51,879 --> 00:22:59,359
of a porter mooning about Barnard’s Inn, under pretence of watching it, fell hollow on my heart.

260
00:22:59,359 --> 00:23:04,558
On the Monday morning at a quarter before nine, Herbert went to the counting-house to report

261
00:23:04,559 --> 00:23:11,840
himself,—to look about him, too, I suppose,—and I bore him company. He was to come away in an hour

262
00:23:11,840 --> 00:23:18,599
or two to attend me to Hammersmith, and I was to wait about for him. It appeared to me that

263
00:23:18,599 --> 00:23:24,438
the eggs from which young Insurers were hatched were incubated in dust and heat, like the eggs of

264
00:23:24,438 --> 00:23:30,919
ostriches, judging from the places to which those incipient giants repaired on a Monday morning. Nor

265
00:23:30,919 --> 00:23:36,240
did the counting-house where Herbert assisted, show in my eyes as at all a good Observatory;

266
00:23:37,240 --> 00:23:42,840
being a back second floor up a yard, of a grimy presence in all particulars,

267
00:23:42,840 --> 00:23:47,719
and with a look into another back second floor, rather than a look out.

268
00:23:47,719 --> 00:23:51,520
I waited about until it was noon, and I went upon ’Change,

269
00:23:51,520 --> 00:23:56,719
and I saw fluey men sitting there under the bills about shipping, whom I took to be great merchants,

270
00:23:56,719 --> 00:24:01,478
though I couldn’t understand why they should all be out of spirits. When Herbert came,

271
00:24:01,478 --> 00:24:06,438
we went and had lunch at a celebrated house which I then quite venerated, but now

272
00:24:06,438 --> 00:24:11,838
believe to have been the most abject superstition in Europe, and where I could not help noticing,

273
00:24:11,840 --> 00:24:17,520
even then, that there was much more gravy on the tablecloths and knives and waiters’ clothes, than

274
00:24:17,520 --> 00:24:23,478
in the steaks. This collation disposed of at a moderate price (considering the grease, which was

275
00:24:23,478 --> 00:24:29,798
not charged for), we went back to Barnard’s Inn and got my little portmanteau, and then took coach

276
00:24:29,798 --> 00:24:35,919
for Hammersmith. We arrived there at two or three o’clock in the afternoon, and had very little way

277
00:24:35,919 --> 00:24:42,680
to walk to Mr. Pocket’s house. Lifting the latch of a gate, we passed direct into a little garden

278
00:24:42,680 --> 00:24:48,719
overlooking the river, where Mr. Pocket’s children were playing about. And unless I deceive myself

279
00:24:48,719 --> 00:24:54,240
on a point where my interests or prepossessions are certainly not concerned, I saw that Mr. and

280
00:24:54,240 --> 00:25:01,000
Mrs. Pocket’s children were not growing up or being brought up, but were tumbling up.

281
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:04,118
Mrs. Pocket was sitting on a garden chair under a tree,

282
00:25:04,118 --> 00:25:08,240
reading, with her legs upon another garden chair; and Mrs. Pocket’s two

283
00:25:08,240 --> 00:25:13,759
nurse-maids were looking about them while the children played. “Mamma,” said Herbert,

284
00:25:13,759 --> 00:25:20,919
“this is young Mr. Pip.” Upon which Mrs. Pocket received me with an appearance of amiable dignity.

285
00:25:20,919 --> 00:25:25,159
“Master Alick and Miss Jane,” cried one of the nurses to two of the children,

286
00:25:25,159 --> 00:25:28,477
“if you go a bouncing up against them bushes you’ll fall over into the river

287
00:25:28,478 --> 00:25:31,840
and be drownded, and what’ll your pa say then?”

288
00:25:31,840 --> 00:25:36,240
At the same time this nurse picked up Mrs. Pocket’s handkerchief, and said, “If that don’t

289
00:25:36,240 --> 00:25:41,879
make six times you’ve dropped it, Mum!” Upon which Mrs. Pocket laughed and said, “Thank you,

290
00:25:41,879 --> 00:25:49,039
Flopson,” and settling herself in one chair only, resumed her book. Her countenance immediately

291
00:25:49,039 --> 00:25:55,000
assumed a knitted and intent expression as if she had been reading for a week, but before she could

292
00:25:55,000 --> 00:26:01,559
have read half a dozen lines, she fixed her eyes upon me, and said, “I hope your mamma is quite

293
00:26:01,559 --> 00:26:09,638
well?” This unexpected inquiry put me into such a difficulty that I began saying in the absurdest

294
00:26:09,638 --> 00:26:15,638
way that if there had been any such person I had no doubt she would have been quite well and would

295
00:26:15,638 --> 00:26:22,240
have been very much obliged and would have sent her compliments, when the nurse came to my rescue.

296
00:26:22,240 --> 00:26:27,240
“Well!” she cried, picking up the pocket-handkerchief, “if that don’t make seven

297
00:26:27,240 --> 00:26:33,879
times! What ARE you a-doing of this afternoon, Mum!” Mrs. Pocket received her property,

298
00:26:33,879 --> 00:26:38,199
at first with a look of unutterable surprise as if she had never seen it before, and then with

299
00:26:38,199 --> 00:26:45,278
a laugh of recognition, and said, “Thank you, Flopson,” and forgot me, and went on reading.

300
00:26:45,278 --> 00:26:50,080
I found, now I had leisure to count them, that there were no fewer than six little Pockets

301
00:26:50,080 --> 00:26:57,319
present, in various stages of tumbling up. I had scarcely arrived at the total when a seventh was

302
00:26:57,319 --> 00:27:04,958
heard, as in the region of air, wailing dolefully. “If there ain’t Baby!” said Flopson, appearing to

303
00:27:04,958 --> 00:27:09,278
think it most surprising. “Make haste up, Millers.”

304
00:27:09,278 --> 00:27:15,039
Millers, who was the other nurse, retired into the house, and by degrees the child’s

305
00:27:15,039 --> 00:27:19,638
wailing was hushed and stopped, as if it were a young ventriloquist with something in its

306
00:27:19,638 --> 00:27:26,080
mouth. Mrs. Pocket read all the time, and I was curious to know what the book could be.

307
00:27:26,080 --> 00:27:31,638
We were waiting, I supposed, for Mr. Pocket to come out to us; at any rate we waited there,

308
00:27:31,638 --> 00:27:36,680
and so I had an opportunity of observing the remarkable family phenomenon that whenever

309
00:27:36,680 --> 00:27:41,559
any of the children strayed near Mrs. Pocket in their play, they always tripped themselves

310
00:27:41,559 --> 00:27:47,919
up and tumbled over her,—always very much to her momentary astonishment, and their own more

311
00:27:47,919 --> 00:27:54,599
enduring lamentation. I was at a loss to account for this surprising circumstance, and could not

312
00:27:54,599 --> 00:28:00,879
help giving my mind to speculations about it, until by and by Millers came down with the baby,

313
00:28:00,879 --> 00:28:06,638
which baby was handed to Flopson, which Flopson was handing it to Mrs. Pocket, when she too went

314
00:28:06,638 --> 00:28:13,118
fairly head foremost over Mrs. Pocket, baby and all, and was caught by Herbert and myself.

315
00:28:13,118 --> 00:28:19,919
“Gracious me, Flopson!” said Mrs. Pocket, looking off her book for a moment, “everybody’s tumbling!”

316
00:28:19,919 --> 00:28:26,840
“Gracious you, indeed, Mum!” returned Flopson, very red in the face; “what have you got there?”

317
00:28:26,840 --> 00:28:29,840
“I got here, Flopson?” asked Mrs. Pocket.

318
00:28:29,840 --> 00:28:34,520
“Why, if it ain’t your footstool!” cried Flopson. “And if you keep it under your skirts like that,

319
00:28:34,520 --> 00:28:40,478
who’s to help tumbling? Here! Take the baby, Mum, and give me your book.”

320
00:28:40,478 --> 00:28:45,759
Mrs. Pocket acted on the advice, and inexpertly danced the infant a little in her lap,

321
00:28:45,759 --> 00:28:50,840
while the other children played about it. This had lasted but a very short time,

322
00:28:50,840 --> 00:28:54,398
when Mrs. Pocket issued summary orders that they were all to be

323
00:28:54,398 --> 00:29:00,919
taken into the house for a nap. Thus I made the second discovery on that first occasion,

324
00:29:00,919 --> 00:29:05,919
that the nurture of the little Pockets consisted of alternately tumbling up and lying down.

325
00:29:06,798 --> 00:29:11,240
Under these circumstances, when Flopson and Millers had got the children into the house,

326
00:29:11,240 --> 00:29:17,438
like a little flock of sheep, and Mr. Pocket came out of it to make my acquaintance, I was not much

327
00:29:17,438 --> 00:29:23,438
surprised to find that Mr. Pocket was a gentleman with a rather perplexed expression of face,

328
00:29:23,438 --> 00:29:28,000
and with his very grey hair disordered on his head, as if he didn’t quite see his way to

329
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,000
putting anything straight. Chapter 23.
